# The device nodes /dev holds, one per line: type major minor name.
# type is c (character) or b (block); the majors are the driver slots in
# ciosw/biosw in sys/consts.c.  create_vol mknod's these onto every new
# volume, so this file is the source of /dev - not a blessed drive.
#
c 0 0 null
c 1 1 console
c 1 1 ttyA
c 1 2 ttyB
c 1 3 ttyC
c 1 16 cent0
c 2 0 mem
c 3 0 io
b 1 0 hda
b 1 1 hdb
b 1 2 hdc
b 1 3 hdd
b 2 8 fla
b 2 9 flb
b 2 10 flc
b 2 11 fld
b 2 12 mfa
b 2 13 mfb
b 2 14 mfc
b 2 15 mfd
b 3 0 m5a
b 3 1 m5b
b 3 2 m5c
b 3 3 m5d
b 3 4 m10a
b 3 5 m10b
b 3 6 m10c
b 3 7 m10d
b 3 8 m16a
b 3 9 m16b
b 3 10 m16c
b 3 11 m16d
b 3 16 m40a
b 4 0 ide0
b 4 1 ide1
#
# The NCR 5380 host adapter.  SCSI is a bus and not a drive, so these name
# targets and not units: the low three bits of the minor are the target's
# id, which is what the select mask is built from, and the type bits above
# them are zero because this card has one board and boards[] (sys/ncr.c)
# has one row.  Nothing here is a drive letter: a target reports its own
# geometry when asked, and what a slice means comes from its label like
# every other disk on this machine.
#
b 5 0 scsi0
b 5 1 scsi1
b 5 2 scsi2
b 5 3 scsi3
b 5 4 scsi4
b 5 5 scsi5
b 5 6 scsi6
b 5 7 scsi7
#
# The whole-disk slice, 'c': the drive at cylinder 0 with no roll, so its
# block 0 is physical block 0 and reading it is reading the label.  The
# minor is the slice and the type in the high bits as everywhere else -
# 72 is slice 2 of the m16 row (2), drive 0; the IDE card has one board,
# so its type bits are zero.  This is the slice label(1) opens.
#
b 3 72 hd0c
b 3 73 hd1c
b 3 74 hd2c
b 3 75 hd3c
b 4 64 ide0c
b 4 65 ide1c
b 5 64 scsi0c
b 5 65 scsi1c
b 5 66 scsi2c
b 5 67 scsi3c
b 5 68 scsi4c
b 5 69 scsi5c
b 5 70 scsi6c
b 5 71 scsi7c
